        "content": [
          "Relative clauses add extra information:",
          "",
          "ENGLISH: The friend WHO helps me...",
          "GERMAN:  Der Freund, der mir hilft...",
          "",
          "Use der/die/das as 'who/which/that':",
          "der - masculine (Der Freund, der...)",
          "die - feminine (Die Freundin, die...)",
          "das - neuter (Das Kind, das...)",
          "die - plural (Die Freunde, die...)",
          "",
          "If it's the OBJECT, use 'den' for masculine:",
          "",
          "Der Mann, den ich sehe, ist mein Lehrer.",
          "(The man that I see is my teacher)",
          "",
          "Relative clauses begin after a comma.",
          "They have a verb in the final position."
        ],
        "pronunciation": "Lektion 34"
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "PREPOSITION + RELATIVE PRONOUN:",
          "",
          "The preposition comes FIRST. The pronoun",
          "takes gender and number from the noun,",
          "and its case from the preposition:",
          "",
          "DAT: dem (m), der (f), dem (n), DENEN (pl)",
          "AKK: den (m), die (f), das (n), die (pl)",
          "",
          "die Milch, aus der man Butter macht",
          "(the milk from which you make butter)",
          "die Finger, mit denen wir Dinge greifen",
          "(the fingers with which we grasp things)",
          "der Freund, für den ich koche",
          "(the friend for whom I cook)",
          "",
          "English can end on the preposition (the",
          "friend I cook for). German never does."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 3,
