    "title": "LESSON 35: INFINITIVE WITH ZU",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "German often uses ZU with infinitives:",
          "",
          "BASIC STRUCTURE: verb + ZU + infinitive",
          "Ich versuche zu schlafen. (I try to sleep)",
          "Es macht Spaß zu tanzen. (It's fun to dance)",
          "Ich habe keine Zeit zu kochen.",
          "(I have no time to cook)",
          "",
          "SEPARABLE VERBS: ZU goes BETWEEN prefix+verb:",
          "aufzustehen (to get up)",
          "anzukommen (to arrive)",
          "",
          "Ich habe vergessen, das Licht auszumachen.",
          "(I forgot to turn off the light)",
          "",
          "NO ZU with modal verbs or lassen!"
        ],
        "pronunciation": "Lektion 35"
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "UM ... ZU = IN ORDER TO (purpose):",
          "",
          "'um' opens the phrase, 'zu' + infinitive",
          "closes it. Both parts need the SAME subject.",
          "",
          "Die Maus kommt heraus, um Essen zu suchen.",
          "(The mouse comes out to look for food)",
          "Ich stelle den Wecker, um früh aufzustehen.",
          "(I set the alarm to get up early)",
          "",
          "OHNE ... ZU = without ...-ing:",
          "Er geht, ohne etwas zu sagen.",
          "(He leaves without saying anything)",
          "STATT ... ZU = instead of ...-ing:",
          "Sie spielt, statt zu lernen.",
          "(She plays instead of studying)",
          "",
          "Always use a comma with um/ohne/statt ... zu."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
